// Question: Why is csrf().disable() appropriate for JWT REST APIs?
// Explain what CSRF is, how Spring Security prevents it,
// and why JWTs make it unnecessary.
// Answer:

solution.code:
// CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) Attack:
// 1. User logs into bank.com — session cookie is set
// 2. User visits evil.com in another tab
// 3. evil.com has: <form action='https://bank.com/transfer' method='POST'>
// 4. Browser automatically sends bank.com's session cookie with the request!
// 5. Bank sees a valid session → processes the transfer

// Spring Security's CSRF protection (Synchronizer Token Pattern):
// - Server generates a random CSRF token, stores in session
// - Includes token in all HTML forms as a hidden field
// - Validates token on every POST/PUT/DELETE/PATCH request
// - evil.com cannot read the CSRF token (Same-Origin Policy)
// - So the forged request lacks the token → rejected

// Why CSRF is NOT a threat for JWT REST APIs:
// 1. JWTs are NOT stored in cookies by default
//    (stored in localStorage or memory — not automatically sent by browser)
// 2. evil.com's forged request won't have the Authorization: Bearer <token> header
//    because the browser won't automatically add it
// 3. Same-Origin Policy prevents evil.com from reading your JWT from localStorage
// 4. So the forged request arrives WITHOUT the JWT → rejected as unauthenticated

// Rule: If you use cookie-based auth → keep CSRF enabled
//       If you use Bearer token auth (JWT in Authorization header) → safe to disable

