// 1. Servlet Container (Tomcat/Jetty)
//    - Has its own filter chain registered at startup
//    - One of these filters is DelegatingFilterProxy named 'springSecurityFilterChain'

// 2. DelegatingFilterProxy
//    - Standard javax.servlet.Filter registered in the servlet container
//    - Bridges the servlet world to Spring's ApplicationContext
//    - Delegates all work to a Spring bean named 'springSecurityFilterChain'
//    - This design lets Spring manage the actual security filters as beans

// 3. FilterChainProxy (the actual 'springSecurityFilterChain' bean)
//    - Holds a list of SecurityFilterChain instances
//    - Finds the FIRST SecurityFilterChain whose requestMatcher matches the URL
//    - Executes that chain's filters in order
//    - Can have multiple chains for different URL patterns:
//      Chain 1: /api/** → [CsrfFilter, JwtFilter, AuthorizationFilter]
//      Chain 2: /admin/** → [CsrfFilter, SessionFilter, AuthorizationFilter]

// 4. SecurityFilterChain (your @Bean)
//    - A list of ordered Security filters to apply for matching requests
//    - You configure which filters are active via HttpSecurity DSL
//    - Key built-in filters (in order):
//      SecurityContextHolderFilter   → loads/saves SecurityContext
//      UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter → form login
//      BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter → OAuth2/JWT
//      ExceptionTranslationFilter    → catches AuthException/AccessDeniedException
//      AuthorizationFilter           → enforces URL-based access rules

// 5. Your custom filters (e.g., JwtAuthenticationFilter)
//    - Inserted via addFilterBefore() at a specific position
//    - JwtFilter runs before UsernamePasswordAuthFilter

// Result: request either reaches your @Controller or is short-circuited
// by a filter (e.g., 401 from ExceptionTranslationFilter)
